1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,163
Someone in a review will eventually ask exactly that, as though it's one question

2
00:00:05,163 --> 00:00:06,113
with one answer.

3
00:00:06,113 --> 00:00:06,945
Is it, though?

4
00:00:06,945 --> 00:00:11,283
It's actually three separate questions, PII handling, data residency, and

5
00:00:11,283 --> 00:00:16,036
training-data rights, each with its own owner, and conflating them is how a team

6
00:00:16,036 --> 00:00:20,849
confidently answers the easy one and never notices the other two went unanswered.

7
00:00:20,849 --> 00:00:25,721
This lecture keeps the three apart on purpose, and gives you the specific question

8
00:00:25,721 --> 00:00:30,534
to ask for each, not a generic reassurance that "we take data privacy seriously."

9
00:00:30,534 --> 00:00:35,525
Picture the meeting where this actually comes up: a customer's security team sends a

10
00:00:35,525 --> 00:00:39,031
questionnaire, or your own legal team asks before a launch.

11
00:00:39,031 --> 00:00:40,991
The question arrives as one line.

12
00:00:40,991 --> 00:00:45,864
The honest answer needs three, and arriving with only one prepared is how a launch

13
00:00:45,864 --> 00:00:48,003
stalls a week before it should ship.

14
00:00:48,553 --> 00:00:53,270
Read the last column, because it's the one that actually matters: three different

15
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:57,929
places, three different owners, and a team that's checked one often assumes it's

16
00:00:57,929 --> 00:00:58,977
checked all three.

17
00:00:58,977 --> 00:01:03,869
PII handling is the one you can answer yourself, because it's about your own system,

18
00:01:03,869 --> 00:01:04,859
not the vendor's.

19
00:01:04,859 --> 00:01:09,692
Trace an actual request: what personal information is in it, and everywhere it goes

20
00:01:09,692 --> 00:01:13,769
afterward, a prompt, a log line, a support ticket someone reads later.

21
00:01:13,769 --> 00:01:17,321
Most teams have mapped this less thoroughly than they assume.

22
00:01:17,321 --> 00:01:19,767
Map it further than the obvious path, too.

23
00:01:19,767 --> 00:01:23,436
The request itself is rarely the only place personal data sits.

24
00:01:23,436 --> 00:01:27,920
A retry queue, an analytics event fired for the product team, an error report

25
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:32,696
captured by a monitoring tool when something crashes mid-request, all of these can

26
00:01:32,696 --> 00:01:37,354
carry personal data along with them, invisibly, well outside the one flow anyone

27
00:01:37,354 --> 00:01:38,403
actually reviewed.

28
00:01:38,953 --> 00:01:44,216
Residency matters most concretely the moment a customer asks whether their data ever

29
00:01:44,216 --> 00:01:49,480
leaves a specific region, a question that's become routine in enterprise procurement

30
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:54,180
and impossible to answer honestly without having actually read the vendor's

31
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:57,939
infrastructure documentation, not just their marketing page.

32
00:01:58,489 --> 00:02:03,272
Notice the bad column's assumption isn't unreasonable, it's just unchecked, and

33
00:02:03,272 --> 00:02:07,025
those are different problems with very different consequences.

34
00:02:07,025 --> 00:02:11,989
Most vendors do offer a setting that stops your data from being used for training.

35
00:02:11,989 --> 00:02:16,832
Plenty of accounts have it left on whatever the default was, which is not always

36
00:02:16,832 --> 00:02:21,311
off, and nobody on the product side ever opened the settings page to look.

37
00:02:21,311 --> 00:02:25,367
The gap isn't malice on the vendor's side or carelessness on yours.

38
00:02:25,367 --> 00:02:30,270
It's a question that felt like someone else's job until a customer's own security

39
00:02:30,270 --> 00:02:31,784
review asked it directly.

40
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:36,869
The fix is almost embarrassingly simple compared to the other risks in this section:

41
00:02:36,869 --> 00:02:41,954
open the setting, read what it actually says today, and write the answer down with a

42
00:02:41,954 --> 00:02:45,525
date, the same discipline 8.1 asked for every register row.

43
00:02:45,525 --> 00:02:50,489
That one afternoon closes a question that otherwise resurfaces every single time a

44
00:02:50,489 --> 00:02:52,668
customer's procurement team asks it.

45
00:02:52,668 --> 00:02:57,330
Notice, too, that a single vendor can answer this question two different ways

46
00:02:57,330 --> 00:03:00,901
depending on which of their products you're actually using.

47
00:03:00,901 --> 00:03:05,926
A consumer chat product and its enterprise API often sit under completely different

48
00:03:05,926 --> 00:03:10,647
data terms, from the same company, and assuming the friendlier consumer answer

49
00:03:10,647 --> 00:03:15,369
applies to the API you're actually calling is its own specific version of this

50
00:03:15,369 --> 00:03:15,853
mistake.

51
00:03:16,403 --> 00:03:21,395
Say the honest caveat plainly, because a course can't responsibly hand you specific

52
00:03:21,395 --> 00:03:25,425
legal answers for every jurisdiction a learner might be sitting in.

53
00:03:25,425 --> 00:03:29,755
What doesn't change by jurisdiction is the shape of the three questions.

54
00:03:29,755 --> 00:03:34,567
Where does personal data go, whose laws govern where it's processed, and who can

55
00:03:34,567 --> 00:03:35,289
train on it.

56
00:03:35,289 --> 00:03:40,220
Ask all three, everywhere you operate, and you've done the part of this job that's

57
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,122
actually yours.

58
00:03:41,122 --> 00:03:45,994
Getting the specific answer right for your specific country and sector is exactly

59
00:03:45,994 --> 00:03:50,866
what legal and compliance are for, and asking them a precise question is far more

60
00:03:50,866 --> 00:03:55,256
useful to them than asking whether the feature is "compliant" in general.

61
00:03:55,256 --> 00:04:00,128
"We're compliant" is the same empty shape as "the model is powerful" from Section

62
00:04:00,128 --> 00:04:05,180
two, or "legal will review it" from two lectures ago: a sentence that sounds like an

63
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:09,811
answer and closes the conversation before anyone's actually checked anything.

64
00:04:09,811 --> 00:04:14,322
The specific, checkable version is always three separate sentences, one per

65
00:04:14,322 --> 00:04:17,149
question, each with its own evidence behind it.

66
00:04:17,699 --> 00:04:22,763
That's the check worth running before anyone else asks you to, and the exercise is

67
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:27,889
more valuable than it sounds, because most people tracing their own feature for the

68
00:04:27,889 --> 00:04:30,729
first time find a stop they'd forgotten about.

69
00:04:30,729 --> 00:04:35,732
A support log, a monitoring dashboard, a debugging export someone downloaded once

70
00:04:35,732 --> 00:04:36,843
and never deleted.

71
00:04:36,843 --> 00:04:38,758
None of these are exotic risks.

72
00:04:38,758 --> 00:04:43,884
They're ordinary infrastructure that happens to be holding personal data in a place

73
00:04:43,884 --> 00:04:46,663
nobody's data-residency answer accounted for.

74
00:04:46,663 --> 00:04:51,356
Do this for the request pattern that carries the most personal data, not the

75
00:04:51,356 --> 00:04:55,988
simplest one, because that's the trace most likely to surface a stop nobody

76
00:04:55,988 --> 00:04:56,667
remembered.

77
00:04:56,667 --> 00:05:01,793
A password reset flow, or a ticket like 4471 with a billing email that differs from

78
00:05:01,793 --> 00:05:06,610
the account email, will teach you more than a generic product query ever will.

79
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:08,923
One thing before you move on.

80
00:05:08,923 --> 00:05:10,381
Trace the request first.

81
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:15,183
You already know your feature better than any document does, so this is usually

82
00:05:15,183 --> 00:05:16,520
faster than it sounds.

83
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:21,443
Then open the actual setting for training-data rights and write down what it says

84
00:05:21,443 --> 00:05:23,631
today, not what you assumed it said.

85
00:05:23,631 --> 00:05:27,947
If it's not what you want, change it, and note the date you changed it.

86
00:05:27,947 --> 00:05:32,748
Next lecture moves to the third row on 8.1's table: which regulations genuinely

87
00:05:32,748 --> 00:05:37,428
apply to your feature, starting with the one everyone's heard of and few have

88
00:05:37,428 --> 00:05:38,279
actually read.
